            <span class="section-kicker">Why Regulated Plants Standardize on GxPHistorian</span>
            <h2>Engineered for FDA Data Integrity Audits—Without Legacy Historian Trade-Offs</h2>
            <p>
              Legacy industrial historians were designed in the 1980s around scarcity of disk space, relying on lossy "Swinging Door" compression and fragile Windows DCOM plumbing. GxPHistorian modernizes every layer for ALCOA+ data integrity.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="diff-grid">
            <article class="diff-card">
              <div class="diff-number">01</div>
              <h3>Mathematically Lossless Compression vs. Lossy Swinging Door</h3>
              <p>
                Traditional historians discard raw sensor readings using lossy <code>CompDev</code> swinging-door straight-line approximations—risking concealed thermal spikes or blunted chromatography UV280 peaks during GMP batch review. GxPHistorian applies <strong>Stage 1 Exception Deadband filtering (<code>ExcDev</code>/<code>ExcMax</code>)</strong> at the edge and archives every emitted sample losslessly in ClickHouse using <strong><code>DoubleDelta</code> + <code>Gorilla</code> + <code>ZSTD(1)</code></strong> at 1.5–2.5 bytes/sample.
              </p>
              <a href="/docs/architecture/hardware-sizing/" class="card-link">See Compression Math &amp; 25-Year Storage Sizing →</a>
            </article>

            <article class="diff-card">
              <div class="diff-number">02</div>
              <h3>Database-Kernel 21 CFR Part 11 WORM &amp; Hash Chaining</h3>
              <p>
                Application-only audit logs can be bypassed by direct SQL access or software bugs. GxPHistorian enforces 21 CFR Part 11 directly inside PostgreSQL 16 PL/pgSQL triggers: every mutation on <code>assets</code>, <code>tags</code>, or <code>event_frames</code> requires <code>app.actor_id</code> and <code>app.change_reason</code>, appends a <strong>SHA-256 hash-chained record</strong> to <code>part11_audit_log</code>, and unconditionally blocks <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>, and <code>TRUNCATE</code>—even by database superusers.
              </p>
              <a href="/docs/architecture/part11-compliance/" class="card-link">Inspect PL/pgSQL WORM Triggers &amp; §11.200 Signatures →</a>
            </article>

            <article class="diff-card">
              <div class="diff-number">03</div>
              <h3>Signal Quality Discipline (<code>quality &ge; 192</code>) &amp; Step Interpolation</h3>
              <p>
                Sensor wire breaks and CIP maintenance cycles routinely emit sentinel values (such as <code>-999.0</code> with OPC Bad quality <code>0</code>). GxPHistorian archives raw diagnostic states while strictly filtering <strong><code>WHERE quality &ge; 192</code></strong> across all 1-minute rollups, LTTB visual trends, Event Frame KPIs, and Excel DataLink queries—and respects <strong><code>LINEAR</code></strong> vs. discrete <strong><code>LOCF</code></strong> step tags so agitator states never interpolate to <code>1.5</code>.
              </p>
              <a href="/docs/clients-and-api/excel-datalink/" class="card-link">Explore Excel DataLink &amp; Interpolation Discipline →</a>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

